    "body": "I also think the [proposal](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5802#issuecomment-801298561) looks good/rational. Thanks @domenic for putting this together so concisely.\r\n\r\n> Dialog gains attribute change steps, which are:\r\n> * If the attribute is open and it's being removed, then run the following subset of the close algorithm:\r\n\r\nI agree with this set of changes - this is parallel to what the [spec already says](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#:~:text=If%20at%20any%20time%20a%20dialog%20element%20is%20removed%20from%20a%20Document) about removing a `<dialog>` from the Document. I would hope that there aren't too many compat issues from this change, given [this warning in the spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#:~:text=Removing%20the%20open%20attribute%20will%20usually%20hide%20the%20dialog).\r\n\r\n> * Being in the shown or shown-modal states does _not_ mean that the dialog focusing steps have necessarily run. So, if you do `dialog.toggleAttribute(\"open\", true)`, this is different than `dialog.show()` because only the latter runs the dialog focusing steps.\r\n\r\nCan you help me understand why we want/need this difference? Any reason not to make `dialog.toggleAttribute(\"open\",true)` just run the same algorithms as `dialog.show()`/`dialog.close()`?\r\n\r\nI also want to point out that I would love to hear more use cases for the `open` attribute. I [opened an issue](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/issues/467) on `<popup>` because it gets even more complex there due to light-dismiss behavior. I know that this ship has likely sailed for `<dialog>`, but I'm wondering if we need to maintain this attribute for `<popup>`. In the meantime, pushing authors toward the `.show()`/`.close()` APIs, and away from mucking with the `open` attribute,  seems like a good thing to do.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
